BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:CFAR Health Access SWG: Language Justice
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261015T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261015T180000
DTSTAMP:20260924T061531Z
UID:00ffad471ed2028d@gid-seminars.wnprc.wisc.edu
CATEGORIES:Fred Hutch Center for AIDS Research (CFAR)
DESCRIPTION:Event interval: Single day event\nOnline Meeting Link: https:/
 /washington.zoom.us/meeting/register/K7Dkwt-2R82qj3ZKU18mCw\nAccessibility
  Contact: g0201@uw.edu\nEvent Types: Lectures/Seminars\n\nRegister here!: 
 \nhttps://washington.zoom.us/meeting/register/K7Dkwt-2R82qj3ZKU18mCw\n\nTa
 lk Title: Increasing Participation of Spanish Speakers with Limited Englis
 h Proficiency (LEP) in HIV Clinical Research in the U.S.\n\nEvent Descript
 ion:  Latinx individuals account for nearly 30% of new HIV infections in t
 he United States yet comprise less than 12% of participants in HIV clinica
 l trials. To evaluate the structural mechanisms underlying this disparity
 \, a qualitative needs assessment was conducted evaluating barriers\, lang
 uage access practices\, and institutional frameworks across clinical resea
 rch sites (CRSs) in the country affiliated with the National Institute of 
 Allergy and Infectious Diseases (NIAID)-funded networks\, including the HI
 V Vaccine Trials Network (HVTN)\, HIV Prevention Trials Network (HPTN)\, a
 nd Adolescent Medicine Trials Network (ATN). This webinar will present the
  preliminary results of this research contrasting ideal federal aspiration
 s—such as the revised NIH Policy and Guidelines on the Inclusion of Wome
 n and Minorities as Subjects in Clinical Research of July 2025\, the Food 
 and Drug Administration (FDA) December 2025 Guidance on broadening eligibi
 lity criteria\, and the statutory protections under Title VI of the Civil 
 Rights Act—against the operational field realities reported by CRS’s r
 esearch personnel.\n\nSpeakers:\nPedro Goicochea\, MSc\, MA is a public he
 alth independent researcher with decades of experience in communications\,
  HIV prevention\, clinical research\, and community engagement across the 
 United States and Latin America. Originally from Peru\, he relocated to th
 e U.S. in 2008 to contribute to the landmark iPrEx study—the first clini
 cal trial to demonstrate the efficacy of pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP) i
 n preventing HIV among men and transgender women. \n\nEvent URL: https://w
 ashington.zoom.us/meeting/register/K7Dkwt-2R82qj3ZKU18mCw\n\nSource: uw_cf
 ar
LOCATION:Online
URL:https://washington.zoom.us/meeting/register/K7Dkwt-2R82qj3ZKU18mCw
BEGIN:VALARM
ACTION:DISPLAY
DESCRIPTION:Reminder: CFAR Health Access SWG: Language Justice
TRIGGER:-PT1H
END:VALARM
END:VEVENT
